###### Abstract

We have identified an optical binary with orbital period P_{b}=4488 s as the probable counterpart of the _Fermi_ source 2FGL J1653.6-0159. Although pulsations have not yet been detected, the source properties are consistent with an evaporating millisecond pulsar binary; this P_{b}=75 min is the record low for a spin-powered system. The heated side of the companion shows coherent radial-velocity variations, with amplitude K=666.9\\pm 7.5 km s{}^{-1} for a large mass function of f(M)=1.60\\pm 0.05 M{}_{\\odot}. This heating suggests a pulsar luminosity \\sim 3\\times 10^{34} erg s{}^{-1}. The colors and spectra show an additional blue component dominating at binary minimum. Its origin is, at present, unclear. This system is similar to PSR J1311-3430, with a low-mass H-depleted companion, a dense shrouding wind and, likely, a large pulsar mass.

